Here's the complete solution. The Flox Catalog tracks nixpkgs with a short lag, so a just-released version like `hello 2.12.2` may not be there yet — but you don't have to wait. You override the existing catalog recipe with a **Nix-expression build** that bumps `version`/`src`, build it, and publish it so it installs like any other package.

## 1. The override recipe

Create `.flox/pkgs/hello/default.nix` (the filename `hello/default.nix` → package named `hello`). This takes the catalog's `hello` and only changes the version and source tarball:

```nix
{ hello, fetchurl }:
hello.overrideAttrs (finalAttrs: _: {
  version = "2.12.2";
  src = fetchurl {
    url = "mirror://gnu/hello/hello-${finalAttrs.version}.tar.gz";
    hash = "sha256-WpqZbcKSzCTc9BHO6H6S9qrluNE72caBm0x6nc4IGKs=";
  };
})
```

> **Hash tip:** if you don't know the hash for a new version, set `hash = "";` first, run `flox build`, and Flox prints the correct hash in the error — paste it back in. (The value above is the real hash for hello 2.12.2, so you can use it directly.)

